00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
vote for us  he led his party to a landslide election  victory in 2019  but boris johnson was to be undone both  by events in which he conspired and also  by his own character flaws that critics  say made him unfit for high office boris  johnson has no social vision he has  there is no moral leadership but for him  it was always about becoming prime  minister not about being prime minister  and he i think he knows himself well  enough that he knows that he does not  have the skills that you need to be a  successful prime minister  sonia pernell was a reporter working  alongside johnson in the brussels bureau  of the daily telegraph she describes a  man with a lot of the child in him who  enjoyed getting into and out of scrapes  a man drawn to chaos and the chaos means  that he can sort of emerge through all  these things going wrong all these all  this noise all this  commotion as the person with the great  idea or the wit the witticism  and that is what appeals to him  for boris johnson the coronavirus  pandemic was a perfect storm of crises  and chaos that blew attention away from  other problems  despite his promise to get brexit done  britain remains mired in dispute with  its biggest trading partner the european  union  the economy is underperforming the cost  of living rising health and education  systems faltering  success with the vaccine program helped  obscure johnson's own indecision and  delays in following scientific advice  that contributed to one of the highest  death tolls in the developed world  former chief advisor dominic cummings  described the prime minister's  leadership as erratic indecisive  distracted nobody  could find a way around the problem of  the prime minister just like a shopping  trolley smashing from one side of the  aisle into the other  the shopping trolley metaphor would  quickly be seized upon by the opposition  so he's doing what he always does  crashing over to the other side of the  aisle boris johnson learned early to be  self-sufficient in a family of  competitive siblings with a mother who  suffered ill health and a father who was  frequently absent  at the elite british boarding school  eaton he came to believe ordinary rules  did not apply to him in the infamous  bullingdon drinking club at oxford the  outward persona of a jovial buffoon  disguised an inner ambition for power  and as both a journalist for the times  newspaper and a minister in opposition  johnson was accused of lying and fired  these then were the qualities he brought  to politics as mayor of london as  foreign secretary and then as prime  minister  entitlement dishonesty and indifference  this is really the first time in his  life uh where he's ever actually been  held to account for anything he's always  been indulged he's always been excused  there's always been reasons made for him  for his bad behavior  there was wide support for his handling  of the war in ukraine but johnson never  recovered from the scandal known as  partygate he received a police fine for  attending a lockdown party in downing  street making him the first british  prime minister to break the law while in  office  a civil service report into a string of  similar gatherings described a failure  of leadership  eventually 148 of his own mps 40 percent  of the parliamentary party turned  against him in a vote of no confidence  boris johnson limped on for a bit  but the reasons to forgive him had run  out jonah al jazeera  let's cross to paul brennan he's joining  us from london so you were at the  announcement that was made by boris  johnson paul talk us through the  statement and the dramatic moment  well the downfall of a prime minister  anywhere i think is always one of a  momentous national occasion and and this  was no different the  first indications that we were going to  get a statement where when the  technicians from downing street came out  and started laying cable  along the road behind me and then from a  couple of doors down from downing street  number 10 downing street uh carrie  johnson and a group of downing street  staffers came out  and stood along the top end of the of  the street and then to my left uh from  another direction from the cabinet  office uh to my left uh a group of boris  johnson's most loyal mps uh came out and  stood here at the gates of downing  street at the far end of the street  there was a large group of the general  public along with demonstrators sending  out music through loudspeakers uh and it  really built up for a um a really a  climactic situation finally the lectern  came out into the middle of the street  and very quickly after that boris  johnson emerged he didn't hang around  there was a round of applause by from  his supporters for him but he didn't  stop to acknowledge that he went  straight into st into the speech that he  had prepared for him and and and  delivered it a short sharp uh speech uh  the reaction  uh from among the commentariat that i've  been looking at on social media in the  wake of that speech are that it was you  know devoid of any self-reflection  frankly there's uh a lot more he could  have said about this short his own  personal shortcomings that have brought  us uh to this uh stage he talks about  the  the decision of the conservative mps to  ask him eccentric which rather suggests  that he thinks they've lost their minds  um that said though there are many  people who will not be mourning the loss  of boris johnson and not least of them  is kirsten who is the labour opposition  leader let's hear  him he needs to go he can't cling on in  this way his own party have finally  concluded that he's unfit to be prime  minister they can't now inflicting on  the country for the next few months it's  obvious he's unfit to be prime minister  that's been blindingly obvious for a  very very long time and if they don't  get rid of him then labor will step up  in the national interest and bring a  vote of no confidence because we can't  go on with this prime minister clinging  on for months and months to come  paul he is staying on for a little bit  longer though what do we know uh is  going to happen in terms of a timetable  and how quickly uh he might be replaced  yeah i mean  that that uh interview with uh kirsten  was recorded before boris johnson's  actual speech was um was given here so  uh the last part uh  overtaken by events i think but the  actual timetable now uh for for the uh  departure of boris johnson is still up  in the air somewhat i mean  the prime minister of britain is not  elected by a general election um the  party takes power through a general  election and then the prime minister is  elected by the members of parliament of  the biggest party from that general  election so it's down to the  conservative party in parliament uh to  uh make the process uh work now  the 1922 committee is the backbench  committee which uh organizes votes of no  confidence for example and it will be  for them  to set the timetable and what we're  looking at most likely is for the  candidates to present themselves and the  expectation is that we could whittle it  down to two leading candidates by the  summer recess which is july the 21st  they could then spend the summer  campaigning amongst the conservative  membership that's around 100 000 members  with a view to holding an election in  the autumn time in time for the annual  conservative party conference um which  is later in the autumn but there are a  lot of misgivings about boris johnson  staying on in downing street while that  process is undertaken dominick cummings  his former closest advisor now his  nemesis since leaving down history he  says that to leave boris johnson here in  downing street could risk risk carnage  and the plenty of other mps who say look  boris johnson was the problem and to  have him  sitting here um overseeing even a kind  of caretaker administration  frankly is not acceptable to them so  there are a lot of questions that are  unanswered still about whether boris  johnson can or will be forced to step  aside so that somebody else can oversee  the transition period through to the  autumn okay thank you so much paul  brennan reporting from london

